From: Omarchy Mobile <omamobile@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: pmi8994_fg: unregister the power supply notifier

The probe registers chip->nb with power_supply_reg_notifier() and nothing
ever unregisters it, so after an unbind (or a probe that fails later)
the global power supply notifier chain keeps a pointer into the freed
chip. The next power_supply_changed() of any supply then calls into
freed memory. On a Redmi 4 Prime (PMI8950, msm8953-xiaomi-markw),
unbinding the fuel gauge and plugging the charger oopsed the events
worker:

  Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address fffffffffffffffc
  Workqueue: events power_supply_changed_work
  pc : notifier_call_chain+0x84/0x1d4
  lr : blocking_notifier_call_chain+0x58/0x8c

and the dead worker left that CPU's per-CPU work stuck for good
(khugepaged waited in __lru_add_drain_all() from then on).

Unregister the notifier with a devm action registered right after it,
so it runs before the cleanup action that disables the works.

---
 drivers/power/supply/pmi8994_fg.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/power/supply/pmi8994_fg.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/pmi8994_fg.c
@@ -906,6 +906,13 @@
 	destroy_workqueue(chip->sram_wq);
 }
 
+static void pmi8994_fg_unreg_notifier(void *data)
+{
+	struct pmi8994_fg_chip *chip = data;
+
+	power_supply_unreg_notifier(&chip->nb);
+}
+
 static int pmi8994_fg_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct power_supply_config supply_config = {};
@@ -1087,6 +1094,11 @@
 				"Failed to register notifier: %d\n", ret);
 			return ret;
 		}
+
+		ret = devm_add_action_or_reset(chip->dev,
+					       pmi8994_fg_unreg_notifier, chip);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
 	}
 
 	return 0;
